1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,096
Good evening from the OROS closing desk.

2
00:00:03,096 --> 00:00:08,978
This morning the system named no pick, so tonight there is nothing to grade.

3
00:00:08,978 --> 00:00:13,700
No setup, no trade, no loss — that is the discipline working.

4
00:00:13,700 --> 00:00:18,619
Why we grade in public: a forecast that is never checked is just content.

5
00:00:18,619 --> 00:00:24,009
Every morning call gets an evening verdict, and both go in the permanent record.

6
00:00:25,800 --> 00:00:29,216
On the day, the book is down 429 dollars.

7
00:00:29,216 --> 00:00:32,048
argenx (ARGX) leads, +0.4 percent.

8
00:00:32,048 --> 00:00:35,047
PayPal Holdings (PYPL) lags at -2.0.

9
00:00:35,047 --> 00:00:39,546
The checklist is clear: nothing requires action today.

10
00:00:39,546 --> 00:00:43,029
On screen, the day's gainers and laggards inside the paper book.

11
00:00:44,480 --> 00:00:52,595
And here is the book itself — the actual holdings, identical to this morning's emailed report.

12
00:00:52,595 --> 00:01:00,882
10 positions, led by Dollar Tree, ticker DLTR, PayPal Holdings, ticker PYPL, Target, ticker TGT.

13
00:01:00,882 --> 00:01:12,623
Every line carries its share count, its entry, and its exit — because a position without a defined exit is not a position, it is a hope.

14
00:01:14,320 --> 00:01:19,067
Now the part that makes this system different: the loop.

15
00:01:19,067 --> 00:01:21,440
Every morning OROS proposes.

16
00:01:21,440 --> 00:01:31,867
Every evening it grades its own call, journals the verdict, and adjusts its own risk before it is allowed to propose again.

17
00:01:31,867 --> 00:01:35,766
Inside the book today: 3 positions up, 7 down.

18
00:01:35,766 --> 00:01:38,055
Best, ADP at +0.51 percent.

19
00:01:38,055 --> 00:01:39,835
Worst, PYPL at -1.98.

20
00:01:39,835 --> 00:01:44,751
Both go in the journal — wins and losses get the same ink.

21
00:01:44,751 --> 00:01:51,872
The honest scoreboard reads: expectancy -2.78% per trade over 1320 graded positions.

22
00:01:51,872 --> 00:01:58,823
Because that record is soft, the system has throttled itself to 50% position size.

23
00:01:58,823 --> 00:02:03,909
Nobody told it to — full size must be earned back with wins.

24
00:02:03,909 --> 00:02:15,946
What tonight's read hands to tomorrow's open: 0 names close the day in Stage 2; position size stays cut to 50% until the record earns it back.

25
00:02:17,360 --> 00:02:19,558
Over to the sports desk.

26
00:02:19,558 --> 00:02:28,627
Day 96 of the race: three books, the same one hundred thousand simulated dollars, since July sixth.

27
00:02:28,627 --> 00:02:34,764
In first place, the live self-rebuilding book, in blue, at 107,985.

28
00:02:34,764 --> 00:02:40,169
Then the index fund, in gray, 7.0 thousand back at 101,028.

29
00:02:40,169 --> 00:02:47,222
Then the frozen first portfolio, the green line, 8.5 thousand back at 99,489.

30
00:02:47,222 --> 00:02:49,787
The race has no finish line.

31
00:02:49,787 --> 00:02:54,000
Same books, same dollars, back at it tomorrow.

32
00:02:54,000 --> 00:02:58,764
Every line starts the same day with the same dollar.

33
00:02:58,764 --> 00:03:00,596
Nothing is restated.

34
00:03:00,596 --> 00:03:02,920
Today's lesson is benchmarking.

35
00:03:02,920 --> 00:03:09,370
Any strategy must beat the boring alternative: the same money parked in an index fund.

36
00:03:09,370 --> 00:03:12,819
That is why the S and P line shares the chart.

37
00:03:12,819 --> 00:03:21,819
If the system cannot outrun it, the index deserves the money, and publishing the race every day keeps the system honest.

38
00:03:23,760 --> 00:03:31,080
All figures are simulated paper trading, graded and journaled by the OROS agents with no human intervention.

39
00:03:31,080 --> 00:03:33,317
Nothing here is financial advice.

40
00:03:33,317 --> 00:03:36,299
From the closing desk — see you at the open.
